  completeExplanation: 'Chapter 6 of Curiosity introduces materials science through observation and hands-on classification. It starts on page 101 with a classical Sanskrit epigraph from Rasaratnasamuchchaya (10.3) noting that crucibles used to melt substances are made of clay and iron. In the classroom, Ghulan and Sheeta explore everyday objects under Madam Vidya\'s guidance. A material is defined as any substance used to create an object. Objects can be made from a single material or multiple materials (e.g. a pen made of plastic, metal, and ink), and a single material can produce diverse objects (wood makes chairs, tables, doors). Classification is the method of grouping objects on the basis of shared properties. The chapter illustrates that material choice depends strictly on properties: a tumbler requires liquid containment, making porous cloth useless; cooking utensils require heat conduction without burning, making paper unviable. Historical context is provided through Lahuradewa (Ganga plains) and Mehrgarh (Baluchistan) pottery dating back 7,000-8,000 years, culminating in Harappan wheel-turned terracotta with decorative slips. The chapter systematically examines physical properties: (1) Lustre: Metals possess shiny surfaces that may dull due to ambient air/moisture; fresh cross-sections or sandpapered surfaces restore metallic shine. Polished plastics shine artificially ("All that glitters is not gold"). (2) Hardness: Soft materials scratch or compress easily with a metal key (sponge, wax, chalk); hard materials resist (iron, stone). Hardness is relative. (3) Optical properties: Ghulan, Sheeta, and Sara\'s hide-and-seek game distinguishes Transparent (light passes clearly; clear glass, clean water), Translucent (light passes hazily; butter paper, frosted glass), and Opaque (light blocked completely; brick wall, wood, metals). (4) Solubility: Sugar and salt dissolve completely in water (soluble), sand and sawdust do not (insoluble), and mustard oil floats as a separate layer (immiscible). Dissolved oxygen in water sustains aquatic life. Homemade ORS is standardized as 6 level teaspoons of sugar and 1/2 teaspoon of salt in 1 litre of water. Finally, Matter is defined as anything having mass and volume. Invisible air is matter because inflating a balloon expands its volume and increases its mass. SI units are standardized: Mass in kilograms (kg), Volume in cubic metres (m³), with 1 m³ = 1000 L and 1 L = 1000 mL. Typography rules mandate lowercase symbols, a space after numbers (7 kg, not 7kg), and no plural "s" (10 kg, not 10 kgs). The chapter concludes with Ayurveda\'s 20 Guṇas (10 opposing pairs from Ashtanga Hridaya 1.18) describing all physical matter.',
